mode: "100644"
text: "# Deriving Pillars\n\nPillars aren't brainstormed from a blank page — they're *mined* from the brand profile, tested,\nand selected. This is the method.\n\n## The five sources (mine each for candidates)\n\nRead `brand-profile.md` and pull candidate themes from each source. Aim wide first — 8–12\ncandidates — then cut.\n\n### 1. Positioning + point of view → the signature pillar\nThe brand's differentiator and its strong beliefs are the richest source. The POV (\"most tools\nare bloated because they're built for enterprises\") becomes the signature theme (\"software that\nrespects small businesses\"). This pillar is what the brand wants to be *known* for.\n\n### 2. Audience jobs-to-be-done + pains → audience pillars\nLook at what the audience is trying to do and what frustrates them. Their *problems* are\npillars; your *features* are not. \"New founders terrified of payroll\" → a pillar about making\nthe scary thing simple. These pillars earn relevance because they're about the audience, not the\nbrand.\n\n### 3. Proof + expertise → credibility pillars\nWhat can the brand teach authoritatively? Where's the track record, the data, the hard-won\nlessons? That expertise is a pillar the brand can own because few others can teach it as\ncredibly.\n\n### 4. The human / brand story → a connection pillar\nThe people, the process, the journey, the values. This is what builds loyalty and makes a brand\nfollowable rather than just useful. For personal brands this is often central.\n\n### 5. Offers / product → at most one conversion pillar\nThe product and offers can anchor *one* pillar, used sparingly. Frame it around the\ntransformation it delivers, not a feature list. Keep it the minority of the mix.\n\n## From candidates to pillars\n\n1. **Generate** 8–12 candidates across the five sources, phrased as **subjects** (\"small-business\n   money mistakes\"), never as intents or formats.\n2. **Test** each with the four-part pillar test (ownable / relevant / strategic / sustainable —\n   see `what-makes-a-pillar.md`).\n3. **Cluster** overlapping candidates into bigger themes (three narrow topics may be one pillar).\n4. **Select 3–5**, ensuring:\n   - exactly **one signature pillar**,\n   - a spread across **audience problems, credibility, and connection**,\n   - at most **one conversion-linked** pillar,\n   - a balance across goals (not all-teaching, not all-selling).\n\n## A fast sanity check on the final set\n\n- Could a stranger read the pillars and immediately understand *what this brand is about*?\n- Does the set include the brand's differentiator (the signature pillar)?\n- Is every pillar about something the **audience** cares about (not just the brand)?\n- Could you fill a month's calendar from these without straining? (If not, a pillar is too\n  narrow — or you have too few.)\n- Is each pillar phrased in language the audience actually **searches**? TikTok, YouTube, and\n  Pinterest are search-driven; a pillar named in the audience's own query language (see\n  `audience-research`'s search-query evidence) compounds there, while internal jargon doesn't.\n- Is promotion the minority?\n\nIf the set fails the \"what is this brand about?\" test, you have intents/formats masquerading as\npillars — go back to the sources and re-derive real themes.\n"
